# PLATFORM-LEVEL. Anything anchored to a directory that belongs to ONE
# implementation lives in that implementation's own .gitignore instead --
# `workspace/*` written here would not match `impl/claude-python/workspace/*`
# anyway, because a pattern containing a slash is anchored to the file it is
# written in. See impl/claude-python/.gitignore.

# Python-generated files
__pycache__/
*.py[oc]
.pytest_cache/
build/
dist/
wheels/
*.egg-info

# Virtual environments
.venv

# Secrets — never commit.
# NOTE: the negation below must keep its ".example" suffix. A bare "!.env"
# un-ignores the real secrets file.
# Unanchored, so it covers every level: the real `.env` now sits in
# impl/claude-python/ beside the compose files that read it.
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local
!.env.example

# conversations
docs/conversations/

# Scratch space -- every temporary/throwaway directory belongs under here.
# NOTE: "temp/*" not "temp/" -- git never descends into a directory excluded by
# the directory form to evaluate a negation, so "!temp/.gitkeep" below would
# silently never take effect (confirmed with `git check-ignore -v` during Task
# 11 verification).
# This one is the PLATFORM's: `ci.py` writes its generated env files and mount
# directories to temp/ci/. The implementation has its own.
temp/*
!temp/.gitkeep

# Second and third implementations, same rule as the first.
impl/*/\.venv

# IDE: `.idea/` IS TRACKED, ON PURPOSE (user, 2026-08-17), so there is
# deliberately no pattern for it here. The nine files under it -- the module
# list, one .iml per build, and vcs.xml -- describe the project a fresh clone
# opens with, which makes them the repository's rather than any one developer's.
#
# A commented-out `#.idea/` sat here and read as an intention nobody had. The
# note beside it was correct and is kept, because it is the reason uncommenting
# would not have worked on its own: A PATTERN DOES NOTHING TO A FILE GIT ALREADY
# TRACKS. Ignoring these would take a `git rm --cached -r .idea` as well, which
# removes them from every other clone -- a decision, not a tidy-up.

# Developer-local MCP servers. `.mcp.json` pointed at a PyCharm MCP endpoint on
# a fixed loopback port, which is one developer's IDE session and not the
# repository's -- it resolves to nothing in any other clone. Untracked
# 2026-08-21, when this repository was prepared for publication. Keep your own;
# it will not be committed.
.mcp.json

# `docs/to-agent-harness/` -- the outbox -- IS TRACKED HERE, ON PURPOSE, and
# that is why there is deliberately no pattern for it (user, 2026-08-21).
#
# It is the record of how this specification was actually negotiated, and this
# repository is where that record belongs. What it must not do is reach the
# PUBLIC repository, because the threads carry the consumer's estate --
# registry addresses, a Maven host, compose paths on their machines -- and a
# provider does not publish those.
#
# **So the removal belongs to the export, not to this tree.** Publishing builds
# a separate two-commit history on the `public` branch, and dropping the outbox
# is a step there. Ignoring it here instead would be the wrong place for it
# twice over: a new thread would silently not be committed, and the private
# record would stop growing without anyone noticing.
#
# The channel rule in CLAUDE.md is unchanged either way: outbound documents are
# written here, their inbox is read-only, and writing to the outbox is
# publishing.
# removes them from every other clone -- a decision, not a tidy-up.

# Developer-local MCP servers -- see the note on the current branch.
.mcp.json

# The outbox: correspondence with the consumer. Not published; it carries a
# third party's internal infrastructure.
docs/to-agent-harness/
